                  <handDesc hands="1">
                     <handNote script="naskh">
                        <p>One hand, probably Persian. Naskh in black ink, with headings and
                           overlinings in red.</p>
                     </handNote>
                  </handDesc>
                  <additions>
                     <p>f. 4r: a Persian sentence and verse, then a faded ownership note of
                           <persName role="fmo" key="">Naṣīr Khān</persName>, probably followed by a
                        price in rupees.</p>
                     <p>Final leaf, verso (pasted to the rear board): Persian ownership note of
                           <persName role="fmo" key="">Sayyid Raḥīm Muslim</persName> (<quote
                           xml:lang="fa">این کتاب مشرف و مکرم / هست در ملک سید رحیم مسلم</quote>).
                        In the same hand, a formal declaration (<foreign xml:lang="fa-Latn-x-lc"
                           >iqrār</foreign>) that this <title xml:lang="fa-Latn-x-lc">Fatāwā-yi
                           Bustān</title> is his property, bought for three rupees, with a warning
                        to anyone who claims it.</p>
                     <p>Same leaf, recto: a list of names with sums in rupees.</p>
                  </additions>
                  <bindingDesc>
                     <binding>
                        <p>Dark blue cloth over leather-covered boards, detached; with a cord for
                           tying it shut.</p>
                     </binding>
                  </bindingDesc>
                  <accMat>
                     <p>(1) English note, probably by library staff (c. 1970): “Incomplete at end.
                        Possibly 18th century …”</p>
                     <p>(2) A Persian letter, 180 × 70 mm, with its fold lines visible: a petition
                        to a patron addressed as <foreign xml:lang="fa-Latn-x-lc">Mullā
                           ṣāḥib</foreign>, asking for the return of money confiscated from one
                        Raḥīm. It names Ghiljī, Kūjī and Raʾūf, and is endorsed <foreign
                           xml:lang="fa-Latn-x-lc">murāsala-yi ʿAbd Allāh …</foreign> The hand is
                        unschooled, with spellings that follow pronunciation (e.g. <foreign
                           xml:lang="fa">دوعا</foreign> for <foreign xml:lang="fa"
                        >دعا</foreign>).</p>
                  </accMat>
